\title{Reconstruction from girth cycles}
\author{Human Compression project: a component of the F012 recovery}
\date{}
\begin{document}\maketitle
\begin{abstract}
When every edge of a graph of degree four belongs to precisely one
girth cycle, the cycles themselves give a smaller graph with a cyclic
order at each vertex. We prove the reconstruction and its isomorphism
criterion. For girth four it reduces the classification on eighteen
vertices to sixteen graphs on nine vertices, each with $3^9$ possible
local orders. A complete finite certificate recovers the known minimum
eighteen and the four extremal graphs. For girth three the same
construction gives the unique extremal graph on nine vertices directly.
These are recoveries of previously published results, not new answers
to open questions.
\end{abstract}

\section{The incidence construction}
All graphs are finite, simple and connected. An $\egr(n,k,g,\lambda)$
has $n$ vertices, degree $k$, girth $g$, and exactly $\lambda$
unoriented cycles of length $g$ through each edge.

\begin{lemma}\label{linear}
If every edge belongs to exactly one girth cycle, two distinct girth
cycles have at most one common vertex.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
They cannot have a common edge, by the hypothesis. Suppose two of them,
$C$ and $D$, contain distinct vertices $x,y$. Choose a shortest $x$--$y$
path in each cycle. Each path has at most $\lfloor g/2\rfloor$ edges,
and the two paths are edge disjoint. Their union contains a cycle: a
forest has a unique path between any two of its vertices. This cycle
has length at most $2\lfloor g/2\rfloor\le g$. It uses edges of both
paths, since each individual path is acyclic. It is therefore distinct
from $C$ and $D$. A length less than $g$ contradicts the girth; length
$g$ puts its edges in a second girth cycle. Both alternatives are
impossible.
\end{proof}

A local cycle system on a simple $g$-regular graph $H$ chooses, at each
vertex $v$, an unoriented cyclic order on the $g$ edges incident with
$v$. Define $M(H)$, with this choice understood, as follows. Its vertices
are the edges of $H$. Two of them are adjacent when they are consecutive
in the chosen cycle at a common endpoint. These are the usual local
cycles in a medial construction; no embedding in a surface is assumed.

\begin{theorem}\label{reconstruct}
Isomorphism classes of graphs $\egr(n,4,g,1)$ correspond to isomorphism
classes of local cycle systems on simple connected $g$-regular graphs
$H$ on $2n/g$ vertices for which $M(H)$ has girth $g$ and each edge
belongs to exactly one cycle of length $g$. For a fixed representative
$H$, the equivalence relation on accepted systems is the action of
$\Aut(H)$ on the local cycles.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Let $X$ be such an edge-girth-regular graph. At a vertex of $X$, its
four incident edges are partitioned into pairs by the girth cycles
through it. Thus precisely two such cycles contain the vertex.
Construct $H$ with one vertex for each girth cycle of $X$ and one edge
for each vertex of $X$, joining the two cycles containing that vertex.
There are no loops. Lemma~\ref{linear} excludes parallel edges. Each
girth cycle contains $g$ vertices, so $H$ has degree $g$.

The incidence graph between vertices and girth cycles of $X$ is
connected: every edge of $X$ lies in one of those cycles. Suppressing
its vertices corresponding to vertices of $X$ gives $H$, which is
therefore connected. The cyclic sequence of vertices in a girth cycle
of $X$ gives the local cycle at the corresponding vertex of $H$.
Under the identification $V(X)=E(H)$, the adjacency in $X$ is exactly
that in $M(H)$. Counting edges of $H$ gives $g|V(H)|=2n$.

Conversely, in a local cycle system on a simple $H$, an edge $uv$ of
$H$ has two neighbors in the local cycle at $u$ and two at $v$.
The two pairs are disjoint: another edge cannot share both endpoints
with $uv$. Thus $M(H)$ is simple of degree four. It is connected
because each local cycle is connected and $H$ is connected.
Every edge of $M(H)$ lies in one of its designated local cycles of
length $g$. The two stated tests say exactly that it is an
$\egr(n,4,g,1)$. In that event the designated cycles are all its
girth cycles, so the preceding incidence construction recovers $H$
and the chosen local cycles without additional choices.

An isomorphism of accepted graphs must map girth cycles to girth
cycles. It therefore induces an isomorphism of their reconstructed
graphs $H$, respecting the local cycles. Conversely any such
isomorphism induces an isomorphism of the medial graphs. After one
representative of each isomorphism class of $H$ is fixed, this is
precisely the asserted automorphism action.
\end{proof}

\section{Two extremal consequences}
\begin{corollary}\label{triangle}
The minimum order of an $\egr(n,4,3,1)$ is nine. There is exactly one
extremal graph: the line graph of $K_{3,3}$, equivalently the $3$ by $3$
rook graph.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
For $g=3$ a local cycle is unique, so $M(H)$ is the line graph of a
cubic graph $H$. Triangles in a line graph come from three edges at
one vertex or from a triangle in the original graph. Indeed, three
pairwise incident edges either have a common endpoint or form a
triangle. Hence the unique-triangle condition is equivalent to $H$
being triangle free.

A triangle-free cubic graph has at least six vertices. To see this,
the neighborhoods of the endpoints of an edge are disjoint, and each
has three vertices. Theorem~\ref{reconstruct} then gives $n\ge9$.
On six vertices, choose a vertex $v$ and its three independent
neighbors. Each neighbor must be adjacent to both of the other two
vertices, giving $K_{3,3}$ and no further choice. Its line graph has
the stated order and the required edge count.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}\label{quadrangle}
The minimum order of an $\egr(n,4,4,1)$ is eighteen. There are exactly
four isomorphism classes at that order.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
We first give a lower bound independent of the finite calculation.
Fix a vertex $v$. Its four neighbors are independent. For a vertex
$w$ at distance two, let $m_w$ be its number of neighbors in $N(v)$.
Counting paths of length two and quadrangles through $v$ gives
\[
 \sum_w m_w=12,\qquad \sum_w\binom{m_w}{2}=2.
\]
The second equality follows because each of the four edges at $v$
lies in one quadrangle, and each quadrangle through $v$ uses two of
them. Since $m-1\le\binom m2$ for every positive integer $m$, there
are at least ten vertices at distance two. Therefore $n\ge15$.
The number of quadrangles is $|E(X)|/4=n/2$, so $n$ is even and
$n\ge16$.

By Theorem~\ref{reconstruct}, orders sixteen and eighteen correspond
to simple 4-regular graphs $H$ on eight and nine vertices, respectively.
For four incident edges there are exactly three unoriented cyclic
orders. They can be specified by the partner of the first edge in
the opposite pairing; the remaining two edges form the other pair.
Thus a graph $H$ on $h$ vertices has exactly $3^h$ local systems.

The finite certificate described below proves the following complete
enumeration. The last column takes orbits under $\Aut(H)$, separately
for each nonisomorphic $H$.
\[
\begin{array}{r|r|r|r|r}
h&\text{graphs }H&\text{local systems}&\text{accepted systems}&
 \text{accepted classes}\\\hline
8&6&39366&0&0\\
9&16&314928&5&4
\end{array}
\]
No order-sixteen graph exists, and the four accepted order-eighteen
classes give all extremal graphs. Each accepted graph is also checked
by a separate enumeration of its quadrangles. This proves both
minimality and the exact number of classes.
\end{proof}

\section{Completeness of the finite certificate}
The certificate uses small graphs from \texttt{geng} as candidates,
but does not assume that its output is complete or free of duplicates.
Both properties are verified by the following independent calculation.

For an ordered degree sequence $d=(d_1,\ldots,d_h)$, let $F(d)$ count
simple graphs on those labeled vertices with those exact degrees.
Invalid sequences have count zero and $F(())=1$. Choosing the
neighbors of the first vertex gives the recurrence
\[
 F(d_1,\ldots,d_h)=
 \sum_{\substack{S\subseteq\{2,\ldots,h\}\\|S|=d_1}}
 F\bigl((d_j-\mathbf1_{j\in S})_{j=2}^h\bigr).             \tag{1}
\]
Each labeled graph has a unique first neighborhood, and deleting the
first vertex leaves exactly the sequence in its summand. Conversely
each residual graph extends in exactly one way for that choice of $S$.
This proves the recurrence by induction. Permuting a degree sequence
does not change its count, by relabeling the underlying vertices.
Sorting the arguments for memoization therefore changes no summand
or multiplicity. The calculated values are
\[
 F(4^8)=19355,\qquad F(4^9)=1024380.                       \tag{2}
\]

For each supplied graph on $h$ vertices, the program explicitly visits
all $h!$ vertex permutations. It encodes adjacency by a bit string,
takes the least resulting string as a canonical representative, and
counts the permutations preserving the original string. The least
strings are pairwise distinct. If the latter count is $a_H$, the
number of labeled graphs represented by $H$ is $h!/a_H$, by the
orbit--stabilizer theorem. The sums of these orbit sizes are exactly
the two values in (2). Distinct orbits already exhaust the independently
counted set, so no graph is missing. All candidates are checked to be
simple and 4-regular. They are automatically connected at these orders,
since every component of a 4-regular simple graph has at least five
vertices. No graph classification or isomorphism oracle is assumed.

The program then visits all $3^h$ local choices and constructs their
medial adjacency matrices literally. A common neighbor of the endpoints
of an edge rejects a triangle. For an edge $uv$ in a triangle-free
candidate, its quadrangles are counted by
\[
 \#\{(a,b):a\in N(u)\setminus\{v\},\quad
 b\in N(v)\setminus\{u\},\quad ab\in E\}.                \tag{3}
\]
These are exactly the cycles $u,a,b,v,u$, with no repetition: the edge
$uv$ and its chosen orientation determine the ordered pair $(a,b)$.
The graph is accepted precisely when (3) is one for every edge.

The full automorphism list from the preceding permutation enumeration
acts on the opposite pairings. If $p\in\Aut(H)$, the two opposite
pairs at $v$ are carried to their images at $p(v)$. Reencoding those
pairs in the sorted neighbor list gives the transformed local choice.
The least base-three choice vector over this complete list is an orbit
representative. This calculation gives four distinct orbits among the
five accepted systems. Theorem~\ref{reconstruct} proves that these are
exactly the graph isomorphism classes, including the possibility of
different cyclic orientations giving the same unoriented cycle.

The retained execution includes every base graph, its least adjacency
string, automorphism count, number of permutations and local choices,
and every accepted medial graph and orbit identifier. A fresh replay
recomputes these data. Independent controls compare (1) with every
labeled simple graph through order six: 33868 graphs and 152 degree
multisets. All five accepted systems receive direct quadrangle enumeration,
and explicit checked bijections match the four classes to the four
previously supplied source graphs. An additional complete execution
with address and undefined-behavior sanitizers has identical output.
Malformed inputs are rejected. These checks are computational evidence
for the displayed proof and its finite enumeration, not Lean proofs.

\section{Attribution and scope}
Goedgebeur and Jooken's fixed June 2024 manuscript, Table 2, reports
$n(4,3,1)=9$ with one graph and $n(4,4,1)=18$ with four graphs.
It attributes the earlier minimum eighteen and its examples to
Drglin, Filipovski, Jajcay and Raiman. The four literal source examples
used here are entries 64--67 of the subsequently retrieved
\texttt{UntilOrder250} list, at repository commit
\texttt{41022f4a3ad84e4502f8889c5b64132225411ca2}; this later list is
not represented as code contemporaneous with the fixed PDF.

The contribution here is the stated reconstruction argument and an
independently complete certificate using smaller incidence graphs.
The incidence and medial constructions, the recurrence for labeled
degree sequences, and orbit--stabilizer counting are standard tools.
No priority is claimed for the construction or the two extremal results.
Neither the reported classifications nor the source's other tables are
used as premises of the enumeration. This component recovers only the
two specified rows. It is not a recovery of all of F012, a numerical
compression measurement, or the resolution of an open conjecture.

